  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nToday through Thursday Night...\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon highlights a pair of \nvigorous shortwave troughs: one spinning over the mid-Missouri \nValley and a second advancing eastward across the lower \nMississippi Valley. Surface analysis reveals low pressure \norganizing along the Florida coast, with an inverted trough \nextending northwestward into the lower Ohio Valley and central \nIllinois.\n\nNear-term RAP/GFS guidance indicates strengthening mid-level \nfrontogenesis over the next 2-3 hours, focused primarily over \nsouthwestern portions of the state and extending northward into\nportions of west-central Illinois. Although 19Z surface \ntemperatures remain above freezing across the area, strong \ndynamic cooling within the f-gen band combined with steep lapse\nrates within the boundary layer will support snow developing \nfor areas along/west of I-55 late this afternoon and evening. \nRAP thermal profiles show sufficient low-level warmth to \nmaintain rain southeast of a Shelbyville-to-Danville line as of \n19Z / 1 PM CST. This warm air will push steadily northwestward \nthrough the evening, crossing the I-55 corridor by 03Z / 9 PM \nCST and reaching the Illinois River Valley near 06Z / Midnight \nCST. Consequently, forecasted snowfall totals have been revised \ndownward across most of central Illinois, shifting the primary \naxis of heavier snow slightly southwestward into the LSX \nforecast area where snow will be prolonged within the pivot \npoint of the wave. By mid-morning Thursday, thermal profiles \nsupport a complete transition to rain area-wide through the \nremainder of the system.\n\nWinter Weather Warnings remain in effect for Scott and Morgan \ncounties, with Advisories continuing for Schuyler, Cass, Menard,\nand Sangamon counties. Snowfall amounts have been trimmed to \n2-5 inches in the warning area and 0-3 inches within advisory \ncounties. Although projected totals fall slightly below standard\nadvisory/warning criteria, headlines are maintained given high \ntravel volumes associated with the holiday period and this being\nthe first notable snowfall event of the season. Note that \nwarming surface temperatures and rain mixing back in by late \nThursday morning will foster rapid melting of any accumulated \nsnow.\n\nFriday through Next Wednesday...\nAs the upper low departs eastward Friday into the weekend, \nprecipitation will clear to our east while temperatures recover \ntoward seasonal normals for Friday and Saturday. Global guidance\n(ECMWF/GFS) has trended towards a more amplified shortwave \ntraversing the region Saturday night into Sunday compared to \nearlier model runs. Light rain will accompany this feature, \nyielding QPF amounts around 0.2 to 0.3 inches. High temperatures\nbriefly drop into the 40s Sunday and Monday behind the passage \nof this trough as high pressure builds overhead. Moderating \ntemperatures return Tuesday and Wednesday with 50s creeping back\ninto the forecast as the ridge axis shifts east of the area.",
